# 10. DYNAMIC PAYMENT GATEWAY MANAGEMENT

THIS IS A CRITICAL REQUIREMENT.

Create a dedicated Payment Gateway Management section in the admin dashboard.

The administrator must be able to manage payment configurations without editing website code.

### Initial supported payment methods

- Paystack.
- Flutterwave.
- Stripe.
- Bank Transfer.
- Cash on Delivery.

Design the architecture to support additional payment providers in the future.

### Add Payment Gateway

Create an Add Payment Gateway interface.

Allow the administrator to select from supported, developer-integrated payment providers.

For each provider, allow configuration of:

- Gateway name.
- Public API key.
- Secret API key.
- Webhook secret.
- Test mode.
- Live mode.
- Enable/disable status.
- Default payment gateway.

### Payment gateway management

The administrator must be able to:

- Add a gateway configuration.
- Edit gateway settings.
- Update API credentials.
- Activate a gateway.
- Deactivate a gateway.
- Delete a gateway configuration.
- Select the default gateway.
- Test payment connection.
- View gateway status.
- View transaction history.

### Dynamic checkout

Do not hardcode payment options into the customer checkout page.

The checkout must retrieve active payment methods from the backend.

When the administrator activates Paystack, customers should see Paystack as an available checkout option.

When Flutterwave is activated, Flutterwave should also appear.

When a payment gateway is deactivated, it must disappear from checkout.

When multiple gateways are active, customers must be able to choose their preferred option.

### Paystack configuration

Support:
- Public key.
- Secret key.
- Webhook secret.
- Test mode.
- Live mode.
- Connection testing.

### Flutterwave configuration

Support:
- Public key.
- Secret key.
- Webhook verification credentials as required.
- Test mode.
- Live mode.
- Connection testing.

### Stripe configuration

Support the required Stripe credentials, webhook signing secret, test mode and live mode.

Only enable Stripe where the merchant account and business are eligible.

### Bank Transfer

Allow administrators to enter:

- Bank name.
- Account name.
- Account number.
- Payment instructions.

Display the configured information to customers selecting bank transfer.

Allow customers to submit proof of payment if enabled.

Administrators must manually verify bank transfers before marking orders as paid.

### Cash on Delivery

Allow administrators to:
- Enable or disable Cash on Delivery.
- Select eligible locations.
- Set additional charges.
- Configure availability.

### Payment security

- Store secret keys securely on the server.
- Never expose secret keys in frontend JavaScript.
- Verify transactions directly with payment providers.
- Verify webhook signatures.
- Validate payment references.
- Prevent duplicate transactions.
- Implement idempotency.
- Keep payment transaction logs.
- Never store customers' card details.
- Never mark orders paid based only on a successful browser redirect.

### Adding future payment providers

Use a modular payment provider architecture.

New payment providers must be integrated through secure backend adapters.

The dashboard must never pretend an unintegrated payment provider is functional simply because its name appears in a dropdown.

The system must clearly distinguish between available integrations and configured gateways.

